WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.279
The "whole new world" that Disney's live-action
Aladdin will take viewers to — is apparently

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.450
the uncanny valley just outside of bizarro
town.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.920
When the House of Mouse unveiled the second
sneak peek at this year's Aladdin, Will Smith

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.920
was finally revealed as the Genie.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.820
"I think people are gonna love it"

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.780
The extended look dropped as a surprise during
the 2019 Grammys on February 10th — and

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.020
it was only a matter of minutes before the
internet lit aflame with reactions.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:30.000
People knew it was coming, but nothing could
prepare the world for what went down in the

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.000
newest Aladdin trailer.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.060
"You really don't know who I am.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.600
Genie, wishes, lamp, none a that ringing a
bell"

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:41.620
Response to the new, blue Genie has been intense,
with countless people reacting in horror,

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:46.220
flaming Smith's look, drawing comparisons
between his character and other blue dudes

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:50.420
from pop culture, and slamming the live-action
Aladdin as a waste of money.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.110
Josh Billinson of the Independent Journal
Review wrote:

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:56.360
"Dammit James Cameron, no one asked for more
Avatar movies."

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:01.120
Kyle Buchanan of The New York Times was downright
horrified at the vision of Smith as the Genie,

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:01.620
tweeting:

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.080
"They premiered this image at night???

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.860
We are supposed to sleep after this???

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:06.270
I have never known peace."

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:10.600
Buchanan went on to admit that his terror
slowly turned to twisted fascination, writing:

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:15.399
"The Will Smith genie makes me feel the same
way I feel when a celebrity dies: Nauseous,

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.440
sad, and like I can't wait to tell everybody
about it.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.360
Did I scream?

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.000
Yes.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.859
Am I pacing around my house now?

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:22.360
Uh-huh.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.280
But am I also filled with […] admiration?

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.260
The ALADDIN movie is batting in the big leagues
now.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.200
There are no other movies until this one and
there may be no other movies after."

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.340
He even joked that Smith as the Genie is what
the never-shown-on-screen monsters of Netflix's

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.200
Bird Box actually look like, posting:

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:43.060
"Honestly the Will Smith genie feels like
a spoiler for BIRD BOX because I finally understand

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:46.509
what image would make them so fascinated but
also want to kill themselves.

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:49.160
I just stepped outside and […] how are there
not crashed cars?

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.499
How are there not people walking around in
a daze?

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.380
Have they not seen the Will Smith genie yet?

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:54.560
Dare I show them?"

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.679
YouTuber Jenny Nicholson shared the same kind
of guttural reaction to Smith's blue Genie,

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:06.180
tweeting,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.979
"Will Smith genie is what appears in my room
when I have sleep paralysis."

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:12.830
Writer and comedian Mike Drucker quipped,
via Twitter,

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:17.049
"This looks like the last character you unlock
in a Final Fantasy game and you rarely use

00:02:17.049 --> 00:02:19.260
him because you've already leveled up the
rest of your party."

00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:23.880
He then offered a chuckle-worthy juxtaposition
of Smith's take on the Genie next to Arnold

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:29.150
Schwarzenegger's Mr. Freeze from 1997's Batman
&amp; Robin, captioning the photo set,

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:33.349
"Me when mom says I can have the house to
myself vs when mom comes home early."

00:02:33.349 --> 00:02:38.549
Other reactions to Smith's Genie include the
many comparisons made to a Smurf, David Cross'

00:02:38.549 --> 00:02:42.939
Arrested Development character Tobias Fünke
 in his Blue Man Group audition get-up, and

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.780
Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson's Scorpion King
from The Mummy 2,.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.960
"I'm afraid I just blue myself,"

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.220
Maybe you're one of the seemingly very few
people who think Smith looks great as the

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.880
Genie, or you're indifferent because…

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.620
"Genie already looked weird in the original
Aladdin.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:02:59.720
Because he's a genie."

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:04.070
Or you're dismayed at the fact that your children
will probably recognize Smith's take on the

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:07.680
character before they ever recall Robin Williams'
OG version.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.640
Say you're a lot smaller than my last master
either that or I’m getting bigger!

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.680
Look at me from the side, do I look different
to you?"

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.180
Either way, there's no avoiding the inevitable.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:20.980
Smith's Genie will bust out of the magic lamp
in the live-action Aladdin soon enough, no

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:26.100
matter if you find him shining, shimmering,
and splendid — or a thing of actual nightmares.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.960
"I was terrified of doing the genie, y'know,
after Robin Williams, y'know"

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.400
"But I found a lane that pays homage, but
it's my own thing"

